{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-33132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at the point where $t=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-33132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨-{e}^{-(0)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{e}^{-(0)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0),\\,-{e}^{-(0)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{e}^{-(0)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0),\\,-{e}^{-(0)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-0+1,\\,-1-0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(0)}{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-33132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcece3a3d2624c5c2cc21a908b0507a8a567e3989bda04ada700b8f5ef127a8e"} {"question" "q-33132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-33132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟩"] ["math" "⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-33132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-33676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve   $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,,\\,2{cos}^{2}⁡t⟩$  at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-33676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,-4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-33676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcece3a3d2624c5c2cc21a908b0507a8a567e3989bda04ada700b8f5ef127a8e"} {"question" "q-33676" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-33676" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-33676" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-75148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}+2)p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=-1$, given that  $p(-1)=i+2\\,j-k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(-1)=i+\\,j-\\,k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-75148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=-1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(({t}^{2}+2)p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2)\\cdot p(t)+({t}^{2}+2)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =2t\\,p(t)+({t}^{2}+2)\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)\\,p(-1)+((-1)^{2}+2)\\,{p}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\,p(-1)+3\\,{p}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =-2(i+2\\,j-k)+3(i+\\,j-\\,k) \\\\  & =-2i-4\\,j+2k+3i+3j-3\\,k \\\\  & =i-j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(-1)}{‖{r}^{′}(-1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (i-j-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ is  $T(-1)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-75148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcece3a3d2624c5c2cc21a908b0507a8a567e3989bda04ada700b8f5ef127a8e"} {"question" "q-75148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-75148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{3}(i-j-k)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(-2i+3j-2k)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}(-2i+j+k)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}(i+2j-k)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-75148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-127219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-127219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨2,\\,2(1),\\,\\frac{1}{1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-127219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcece3a3d2624c5c2cc21a908b0507a8a567e3989bda04ada700b8f5ef127a8e"} {"question" "q-127219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-127219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2}⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{2}{3},2,-\\frac{1}{3}⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-127219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-127274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, given that  $p(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=i-\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\sqrt{3}\\,k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-127274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)\\cdot p(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t\\,p(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})p(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){p}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2(i-\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k)+(1)(\\sqrt{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =(2\\,i-3\\,j+\\sqrt{3}\\,k)+\\sqrt{3}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+2\\sqrt{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9+12} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (2\\,i-3\\,j+2\\sqrt{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n $T(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{2}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/state.json" "file_sha256" "8880ac2bce614f79757d54ef63bf2effc05f34e64abd20e8cde2c8cdf4497e5b"} {"question" "q-127274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcece3a3d2624c5c2cc21a908b0507a8a567e3989bda04ada700b8f5ef127a8e"} {"question" "q-127274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-